# SfM Reconstruction

This directory is the clean implementation of the project. The legacy
notebooks and scripts under `../Experiments` and `../project_3dcv-main` are
kept as reference material and are not imported.

Supported workflows:

- Stage 1: calibrated incremental SfM from supplied correspondences.
- Stage 2: SIFT detection, custom descriptor matching, geometric filtering,
  then the same incremental reconstruction backend.

## Environment

An existing environment on this machine already contains the required runtime:

```powershell
$python = "C:\Users\mopu01\AppData\Local\anaconda3\envs\mardm\python.exe"
```

To install the package into another environment:

```powershell
python -m pip install -e .
```

## Run Stage 1

From this directory:

```powershell
$env:PYTHONPATH = "src"
& $python -m sfm_reconstruction `
  --dataset "..\Experiments\Stage_1_Data_ver._4\Stage_1_Data_ver_4\stage1\box" `
  --output-dir "outputs\stage1_box"
```

## Run Stage 2

Milk validation:

```powershell
$env:PYTHONPATH = "src"
& $python -m sfm_reconstruction `
  --stage 2 `
  --dataset "..\Experiments\Stage_2_Data\stage2\milk" `
  --output-dir "outputs\stage2_milk"
```

Boot submission:

```powershell
& $python -m sfm_reconstruction `
  --stage 2 `
  --dataset "..\Experiments\Stage_2_Data\stage2\boot" `
  --output-dir "outputs\stage2_boot_improved" `
  --max-features 2500 `
  --mask-apriltags `
  --min-track-observations 3 `
  --max-point-distance-factor 1.5 `
  --bundle-adjustment-max-nfev 20
```

SIFT features and filtered correspondences are cached under each output
directory, so repeated runs do not repeat matching unless
`--overwrite-correspondences` is supplied.

The Stage 2 defaults deliberately use a strict three-neighbor image graph:
1,500 SIFT features, a 0.75 mutual ratio test, calibrated geometric filtering,
and at least 15 inliers per accepted image pair.

For the supplied Stage 2 scenes, `--mask-apriltags` prevents the calibration
sheet from consuming most of the SIFT feature budget. Requiring three-view
tracks removes weak two-view geometry, while
`--max-point-distance-factor 1.5` rejects points far outside the camera loop
before and after bundle adjustment. Use `--min-track-observations 4` for a
smaller high-confidence cloud.

For a faster diagnostic run:

```powershell
& $python -m sfm_reconstruction `
  --dataset "..\Experiments\Stage_1_Data_ver._4\Stage_1_Data_ver_4\stage1\box" `
  --output-dir "outputs\stage1_box_no_ba" `
  --no-bundle-adjustment
```

The command writes:

- `estimated_camera_parameters.json`
- `estimated_points.ply`
- `estimated_points_rich.ply`, with RGB, track IDs, observation counts,
  reprojection error, and triangulation-angle attributes
- `summary.json`, including scale-normalized pose metrics when ground truth exists

The final verified version-4 box regression registered all 46 cameras and
reconstructed 5,746 sparse points. With 20 bundle-adjustment evaluations, it
reached about 0.88 degrees mean rotation error and 0.067 mean translation
error under the provided evaluator's scale convention.

The balanced improved milk run registered all 50 cameras and reconstructed
1,739 three-view points. Mean rotation error improved from 7.83 to 6.78
degrees, mean translation error improved from 0.070 to 0.063, and mean
point-to-mesh vertex distance improved from 0.067 to 0.020 in the validation
coordinate frame. The four-view variant reached 6.51 degrees and 0.016 mean
point-to-mesh distance with 961 points.

The improved boot run registered all 51 cameras and reconstructed 1,254
high-confidence points. Its deliverables and Blender scene are under
`outputs/stage2_boot_improved`.

## Visualize Results

Save a PNG:

```powershell
$env:PYTHONPATH = "src"
& $python -m sfm_reconstruction.visualize `
  --result-dir "outputs\stage2_boot_improved"
```

This creates `outputs\stage2_boot_improved\reconstruction.png`.

Open an interactive 3D window that can be rotated and zoomed:

```powershell
& $python -m sfm_reconstruction.visualize `
  --result-dir "outputs\stage2_boot_improved" `
  --show
```

Use `outputs\stage2_milk_masked_track3_ba` or
`outputs\stage1_box_filter_regression` to view the other verified
reconstructions.

### Open3D

Install the optional Open3D dependency when needed:

```powershell
python -m pip install -e .[open3d]
```

Open the colored point cloud with reconstructed camera trajectory and
frustums:

```powershell
$env:PYTHONPATH = "src"
& $python -m sfm_reconstruction.open3d_viewer `
  --result-dir "outputs\stage2_boot_improved" `
  --color-mode rgb
```

Available color modes are `rgb`, `track_length`, `reprojection_error`,
`triangulation_angle`, and `height`.

### Blender

Blender 4.4 is installed on this machine. Create a ready-to-open scene with
visible sparse points, reconstructed camera frustums, and the camera
trajectory:

```powershell
& $python -m sfm_reconstruction.blender_viewer `
  --result-dir "outputs\stage2_boot_improved" `
  --open
```

The command saves `outputs\stage2_boot_improved\reconstruction.blend` before
opening it. Omit `--open` to only generate the file. Inside Blender, use the
middle mouse button to orbit, the mouse wheel to zoom, and press `Home` to
frame the complete reconstruction.

The importer trims the outermost one percent of points on each axis for a
clearer view. Use `--trim-percentile 0` to include every reconstructed point,
or `--point-size 0.01` to set marker size explicitly.

